<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Kuyavia</h1><p class="st-meta">1233 CE – 1267 CE (approx.) · capital Inowroclaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Piast duchy on the lower Vistula whose ruler Casimir I fathered the dukes who later reunited Poland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, the fertile land around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-inowroclaw">Inowroclaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruszwica">Kruszwica</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-masovia">Duchy of Masovia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I</a>. In 1233 Konrad gave it to his son Casimir, who made it a separate duchy and later added <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-leczyca">Leczyca</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-sieradz">Sieradz</a> to his lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> was rich in farmland and salt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruszwica">Kruszwica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Wloclawek</a>, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Kuyavia, were important centres. Bordering the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, the duchy faced frequent disputes over land and trade. Casimir I fought his neighbours for influence in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Casimir I died in 1267 his lands were divided among his sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek the Black</a> received <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-sieradz">Sieradz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziemomysl-of-inowroclaw">Ziemomysl</a> took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-inowroclaw">Duchy of Inowroclaw</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-of-leczyca">Casimir II</a> shared the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brzesc-kujawski">Duchy of Brzesc Kujawski</a>. Wladyslaw later reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-kuyavia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Kuyavia" width="320" height="281"><figcaption>Image: Hoodinski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_in_poland_12-13c.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1233 CE – 1267 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Inowroclaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Casimir I of Kuyavia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kujawy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=duchy-of-kuyavia" aria-label="Duchy of Kuyavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5089 358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Kuyavia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5409 614L5442 621M5409 614L5409 614M5409 614L5424 620"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5442 621h0M5409 614h0M5424 620h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5409 614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5409 614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=duchy-of-kuyavia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
